{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 5,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.05,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.087,
            0.634,
            0.109
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Examine whether ∆ABE and ∆ABF are congruent."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.119,
            0.847,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": "For this, you could use one or more of the following methods — tracing and comparing, taking a cutout and superimposing, or observing that AB acts as a line of symmetry due to the ‘sameness’ of the act of construction above and below this line."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.196,
            0.847,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": "We see that ∆ABE and ∆ABF are congruent. From this general construction, we can see that all triangles with the same sidelengths are congruent. Hence, Meera was right when she said that the sidelengths are sufficient to construct a congruent triangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.272,
            0.522,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "Thus, we have the following result:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.294,
            0.846,
            0.337
          ],
          "content": "**If two triangles have the same sidelengths, then they are congruent.** We call this the **SSS (Side Side Side)** condition for congruence."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.354,
            0.596,
            0.376
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Conventions to Express Congruence"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.381,
            0.847,
            0.421
          ],
          "content": "The two triangles given below are congruent. How can these two triangles be superimposed? Which vertices of ∆XYZ and ∆ABC should we overlap?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.428,
            0.801,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": "This has to be done so that the equal sides overlap. Figure out how."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.319,
            0.463,
            0.68,
            0.591
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.598,
            0.847,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": "Overlapping Vertex A over Vertex X, Vertex B over Vertex Y and Vertex C over Vertex Z will ensure that equal sides overlap, making the triangles fit exactly over each other."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.665,
            0.846,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": "？ Are there other ways of overlapping the vertices so that the triangles fit exactly over each other?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.717,
            0.847,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": "The fact that these triangles are congruent shows that their respective angles are equal:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.348,
            0.766,
            0.652,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{A} = \\angle \\mathrm{X}, \\angle \\mathrm{B} = \\angle \\mathrm{Y}   and   \\angle \\mathrm{C} = \\angle \\mathrm{Z}",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.795,
            0.847,
            0.853
          ],
          "content": "Thus, when two triangles are congruent, there are corresponding vertices, sides and angles which fit exactly over each other when the triangles are made to overlap. In this case, they are"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.856,
            0.697,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": "(a) Corresponding Vertices: A and X, B and Y, C and Z"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.935,
            0.182,
            0.95
          ],
          "content": "6"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
